workedExample:
1. `read(fd, buf, 65536)` means *up to* 65,536 bytes.
2. A successful read can return the 1,200 bytes available or transferred so far; a read boundary is not a TCP-segment boundary.
3. Continue until the frame is complete, or handle premature EOF/error. The missing bytes are not guaranteed to arrive.
4. Correct pattern:

```c
size_t got = 0;
/* A read may deliver only part. */
while (got < len) {
  ssize_t n = read(fd, buf + got,
                   len - got);
  if (n == 0) break;   /* EOF */
  if (n < 0) {
    if (errno == EINTR) continue;
    return -1;
  }
  /* Next read starts after these */
  /* bytes. */
  got += n;
}
```

The `EINTR` check handles a signal interrupting the call.
